(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[453],{5121:(e,t,n)=>{"use strict";n.d(t,{A:()=>l});var a=n(12115);let s=function(){for(var e=arguments.length,t=Array(e),n=0;n<e;n++)t[n]=arguments[n];return t.filter((e,t,n)=>!!e&&""!==e.trim()&&n.indexOf(e)===t).join(" ").trim()},i=e=>{let t=e.replace(/^([A-Z])|[\s-_]+(\w)/g,(e,t,n)=>n?n.toUpperCase():t.toLowerCase());return t.charAt(0).toUpperCase()+t.slice(1)};var r={xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",width:24,height:24,viewBox:"0 0 24 24",fill:"none",stroke:"currentColor",strokeWidth:2,strokeLinecap:"round",strokeLinejoin:"round"};let o=(0,a.forwardRef)((e,t)=>{let{color:n="currentColor",size:i=24,strokeWidth:o=2,absoluteStrokeWidth:l,className:h="",children:d,iconNode:c,...u}=e;return(0,a.createElement)("svg",{ref:t,...r,width:i,height:i,stroke:n,strokeWidth:l?24*Number(o)/Number(i):o,className:s("lucide",h),...!d&&!(e=>{for(let t in e)if(t.startsWith("aria-")||"role"===t||"title"===t)return!0;return!1})(u)&&{"aria-hidden":"true"},...u},[...c.map(e=>{let[t,n]=e;return(0,a.createElement)(t,n)}),...Array.isArray(d)?d:[d]])}),l=(e,t)=>{let n=(0,a.forwardRef)((n,r)=>{let{className:l,...h}=n;return(0,a.createElement)(o,{ref:r,iconNode:t,className:s("lucide-".concat(i(e).replace(/([a-z0-9])([A-Z])/g,"$1-$2").toLowerCase()),"lucide-".concat(e),l),...h})});return n.displayName=i(e),n}},52987:(e,t,n)=>{"use strict";n.d(t,{A:()=>a});let a=(0,n(5121).A)("arrow-right",[["path",{d:"M5 12h14",key:"1ays0h"}],["path",{d:"m12 5 7 7-7 7",key:"xquz4c"}]])},53453:(e,t,n)=>{Promise.resolve().then(n.bind(n,90454)),Promise.resolve().then(n.t.bind(n,52619,23))},86651:(e,t,n)=>{"use strict";n.d(t,{A:()=>a});let a=(0,n(5121).A)("search",[["path",{d:"m21 21-4.34-4.34",key:"14j7rj"}],["circle",{cx:"11",cy:"11",r:"8",key:"4ej97u"}]])},90454:(e,t,n)=>{"use strict";n.d(t,{PostIndex:()=>u});var a=n(95155),s=n(12115),i=n(52619),r=n.n(i),o=n(86651),l=n(52987);let h=e=>"https://github.com/PujanCoder/".concat(e),d=[{slug:"gpt2-from-scratch-tensor-shapes",kind:"blog",title:"Writing GPT-2 from scratch: what the tensor shapes teach you",summary:"Rebuilding GPT-2 without the transformers library turns an architecture diagram into a list of shape constraints. A walkthrough of the module order and the two mistakes that shape-checking catches.",category:"Deep learning",date:"2026-02-02",readingMinutes:8,tags:["GPT-2","PyTorch","transformers","attention"],body:'## Why rebuild it\n\nYou can use a transformer without understanding it. Importing a pretrained model and calling `generate` is a legitimate engineering choice, and most of the time it is the right one.\n\nBuilding it once is still worth doing, because an architecture diagram is a picture and a shape is a constraint. The moment you write the attention block yourself, the diagram stops being optional.\n\n## The module order\n\nGPT-2 small, as implemented:\n\n1. Token embedding, 50,257 x 768, plus a learned position embedding of the same width.\n2. Twelve identical blocks. Each block is layer norm, causal self-attention, residual add, layer norm, MLP, residual add.\n3. Final layer norm.\n4. Language-model head, weight-tied to the token embedding.\n\nThe pre-norm ordering matters. Normalising before each sublayer keeps the residual stream clean, which is what lets twelve layers stack without the training instabilities that post-norm transformers hit.\n\n## Shape check one: the head split\n\nAttention projects to 768 dimensions, then reshapes to 12 heads of 64. If you reshape the wrong way, the model still runs. It just attends across a scrambled subspace.\n\nThe correct reshape is (batch, seq, 768) to (batch, seq, 12, 64) then transpose to (batch, 12, seq, 64). Transposing before splitting the head dimension silently mixes features across heads. Nothing errors. Accuracy quietly degrades.\n\nThat is the single most useful thing a from-scratch implementation teaches: some bugs have no exception.\n\n## Shape check two: the causal mask\n\nA causal mask is an upper-triangular block of negative infinity added to the attention scores before softmax. Two details:\n\n- It must be added before the softmax, not after, or the probabilities will not sum to one.\n- It must broadcast over the head dimension, not the batch dimension.\n\nA test that asserts "position 0 cannot attend to position 1" catches both. Without that test, a wrong mask looks like a model that learns slightly worse, which is indistinguishable from a learning-rate problem.\n\n## What is deliberately not finished\n\nThe repository ships training and generation entry points as scaffolding. There is no checkpoint, and the README says so. A faithful architecture without training proves structure, not language ability.\n\nThe value is in the six pytest modules: attention causality, embedding shapes, the MLP, the transformer block, tokenizer round-trips, and an end-to-end run. Those are the parts that can be verified without a GPU.\n\n## If you want to do the same\n\nStart with the tokenizer. Byte-level BPE is where most of the surprising behaviour lives, because it decides what a "word" is before the model sees a single number. Then attention, then the block, then the head. Write the shape test before the module.',evidence:[{label:"GPT-2 clone",href:h("GPT-2-CLONE")},{label:"Attention module",href:"".concat(h("GPT-2-CLONE"),"/blob/main/model/attention.py")},{label:"Run the tokenizer lab",href:"/labs/gpt2-clone/"}]},{slug:"the-feature-contract",kind:"blog",title:"The feature contract that stops training and serving from drifting",summary:"Most silent ML regressions are not model bugs. They are two copies of a feature transform that stopped agreeing. A short account of the contract test that fails the build instead.",category:"MLOps",date:"2026-01-25",readingMinutes:5,tags:["feature engineering","testing","MLOps","sklearn"],body:"## The failure mode\n\nA notebook computes 23 features. A service computes the same 23 features, written six months later by the same person, in a different file. Both run. Neither errors.\n\nThen the model's precision drops from 0.96 to 0.71 and nobody knows why, because the only visible symptom is a number on a dashboard.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nFeature code is copied, not shared. The copy drifts in small ways:\n\n- A missing value filled with 0 in one place and the column mean in the other.\n- A duration in milliseconds in training, seconds in serving.\n- One extra column appended at the end, shifting nothing but changing the matrix width.\n\nNone of these are exceptions. All of them change the input distribution.\n\n## The fix: one extractor, one test\n\nThe NDR platform imports a single extractor module from both the training pipeline and the API. The test asserts that the saved artefact's expected column list equals the extractor's output for a fixture row, in order and with matching dtypes.\n\n```python\ndef test_feature_contract(model_metadata, fixture_flow):\n    features = extract_features(fixture_flow)\n    assert list(features.keys()) == model_metadata[\"feature_names\"]\n    assert len(features) == 23\n```\n\nIf someone reorders the columns or adds a feature without retraining, CI fails. That is the whole trick: make the drift a build error instead of a production mystery.\n\n## What it does not catch\n\n- **Semantic drift.** If the source system starts reporting duration in a different unit, the contract still passes. Only distribution monitoring catches that.\n- **Leakage.** A feature computed from the label passes every shape test.\n- **Preprocessing drift.** Scaling is fitted on training data; if the serving path refits, the contract holds and the numbers still move.\n\nSo the contract is necessary and not sufficient. It removes one class of silent failure, which is the class that happens most often.\n\n## The general principle\n\nEvery boundary between a training environment and a serving environment is a place where two copies of the same logic can disagree. Find those boundaries and put an assertion on them. The assertion is cheaper than the incident.",evidence:[{label:"Contract test",href:"".concat(h("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps"),"/blob/main/tests/test_feature_contract.py")},{label:"Serving extractor",href:"".concat(h("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps"),"/blob/main/src/realtime/features.py")}]},{slug:"reading-a-confusion-matrix",kind:"blog",title:"Reading a confusion matrix like an engineer",summary:"Accuracy is a summary. A confusion matrix is a description. A practical walkthrough of the four cells, which one costs money, and how to pick a threshold that matches the cost of being wrong.",category:"Evaluation",date:"2026-01-12",readingMinutes:6,tags:["evaluation","metrics","thresholds"],body:"## Four cells, two kinds of mistake\n\nA binary classifier produces four outcomes:\n\n- **True positive** — flagged, and it was real.\n- **False positive** — flagged, and it was not. This costs analyst time.\n- **False negative** — not flagged, and it was real. This costs an incident.\n- **True negative** — not flagged, and it was not. This is the boring majority.\n\nAccuracy collapses all four into one number, which means it treats the two mistakes as equally expensive. They almost never are.\n\n## Precision and recall are cost statements\n\n**Precision** = TP / (TP + FP). Of everything you flagged, how much was real. Low precision means your team is chasing noise.\n\n**Recall** = TP / (TP + FN). Of everything real, how much you caught. Low recall means incidents you never saw.\n\nYou cannot maximise both. The threshold is the dial between them, and choosing it is a product decision disguised as a hyperparameter.\n\n## A worked example\n\nThe NDR model at threshold 0.95, on 44,734 held-out flows:\n\n- precision 0.963, recall 0.983, F1 0.973, false-positive rate 3.56%.\n\nDrop the threshold to 0.5 and recall climbs toward 1.0 while precision falls and the alert queue fills with benign flows. The model did not change. The operating point did.\n\nThat is why the platform fixes the threshold against a false-positive budget instead of maximising accuracy: the budget is a staffing constraint, and the model should respect it.\n\n## The medical version\n\nThe chest X-ray pipeline has the opposite cost structure. A missed tuberculosis case is far worse than a false alarm, so the gate enforces a recall floor of 0.80 and will reject a candidate that improves accuracy while missing cases.\n\nA 0.867-accuracy model was rejected at 0.236 positive-class recall. On accuracy alone it looked fine. On the matrix it was dangerous.\n\n## How to choose a threshold\n\n1. Write down the cost of a false positive and a false negative, in whatever units you have (minutes, currency, risk).\n2. Sweep the threshold and compute both counts at each point.\n3. Pick the point where the marginal cost of one more false positive equals the marginal benefit of one fewer false negative.\n4. Write the choice down, because the next person will otherwise re-tune it by feel.\n\nThe matrix is not a diagnostic. It is a ledger.",evidence:[{label:"NDR operating-point lab",href:"/labs/network-intrusion-detection/"},{label:"Medical gate lab",href:"/labs/medical-imaging-mlops/"},{label:"Playground confusion matrix",href:"/playground/"}]},{slug:"byte-level-bpe-step-by-step",kind:"blog",title:"Byte-level BPE, step by step",summary:"How GPT-2 turns text into 50,257 possible tokens: a regex pre-tokeniser, a reversible byte map, and an ordered merge table applied by rank. Includes the reason tokenisation is not a preprocessing detail.",category:"NLP",date:"2025-12-30",readingMinutes:7,tags:["tokenization","BPE","GPT-2","NLP"],body:'## The problem tokenisation solves\n\nA neural network needs fixed-size integer inputs. Text is a variable-length sequence of unicode code points. Something has to map one to the other, and that something decides what the model can see.\n\nWord-level vocabularies choke on typos and rare words. Character-level vocabularies make sequences long and attention expensive. Byte-level BPE sits between them: common strings become single tokens, rare strings fall back to bytes.\n\n## Step 1: pre-tokenise\n\nGPT-2 splits text with a regex before any merging, roughly: contractions, letter runs, number runs, punctuation runs, and whitespace. Pre-tokenisation prevents merges from crossing word boundaries, so "the cat" never becomes a single token.\n\n## Step 2: map bytes to printable unicode\n\nThere are 256 possible bytes, and some of them are unprintable control characters. GPT-2 maps each byte to a printable unicode symbol with a reversible table, so the tokenizer can operate on strings while remaining lossless for arbitrary UTF-8.\n\nThat reversibility is the point: decode(encode(text)) == text for any input, including emoji and invalid sequences.\n\n## Step 3: apply merges by rank\n\nThe merge table is an ordered list of byte-pair merges learned on a corpus. Encoding a string means:\n\n1. Start with the byte sequence for each pre-token.\n2. Find the pair with the lowest merge rank.\n3. Merge it. Repeat until no pair has a rank.\n\nThe order is the model. "low" and "er" merge into "lower" only if that pair has a rank, and the rank was learned from frequency.\n\n## Why this is not a preprocessing detail\n\nTokenisation determines:\n\n- **Sequence length**, and therefore attention cost.\n- **What counts as a unit of meaning.** " 2026" with a leading space is a different token from "2026".\n- **Robustness.** Byte fallback means no input is ever out-of-vocabulary, but a rare word costs many tokens.\n\nA model that is "bad at numbers" is often a tokenizer that splits numbers into inconsistent chunks. The architecture is not at fault; the vocabulary is.\n\n## Try it\n\nThe tokenizer lab trains a small merge table in the browser from a fixed corpus, so you can watch the pairs collapse. The repository version ships the real 50,257-token vocabulary and merge table, which is why its token IDs match GPT-2\'s rather than approximating them.',evidence:[{label:"BPE implementation",href:"".concat(h("GPT-2-CLONE"),"/blob/main/tokenizer/bpe.py")},{label:"Tokenizer lab",href:"/labs/gpt2-clone/"},{label:"NLP playground",href:"/playground/"}]}],c=e=>"https://github.com/PujanCoder/".concat(e);function u(e){let{posts:t,kind:n}=e,[i,h]=(0,s.useState)(""),[d,c]=(0,s.useState)("all"),u=(0,s.useMemo)(()=>Array.from(new Set(t.map(e=>e.category))).sort(),[t]),m=(0,s.useMemo)(()=>{let e=i.trim().toLowerCase();return t.filter(t=>("all"===d||t.category===d)&&(!e||[t.title,t.summary,t.category,...t.tags].join(" ").toLowerCase().includes(e)))},[t,i,d]);return(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("label",{className:"relative block w-full sm:max-w-xs",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"sr-only",children:["Search ","research"===n?"research notes":"articles"]}),(0,a.jsx)(o.A,{className:"pointer-events-none absolute left-3 top-1/2 h-3.5 w-3.5 -translate-y-1/2 text-quiet","aria-hidden":"true"}),(0,a.jsx)("input",{value:i,onChange:e=>h(e.target.value),placeholder:"Search titles, tags, topics…",className:"w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-white py-2 pl-9 pr-3 text-[12.5px] text-ink outline-none placeholder:text-quiet/70 focus:border-emerald-500"})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap gap-1.5",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("button",{type:"button",onClick:()=>c("all"),"aria-pressed":"all"===d,className:"rounded-full border px-2.5 py-1 text-[11px] transition-colors ".concat("all"===d?"border-forest bg-forest text-white":"border-line bg-white text-quiet hover:border-emerald-300 hover:text-forest"),children:["All ",t.length]}),u.map(e=>(0,a.jsx)("button",{type:"button",onClick:()=>c(e),"aria-pressed":d===e,className:"rounded-full border px-2.5 py-1 text-[11px] transition-colors ".concat(d===e?"border-forest bg-forest text-white":"border-line bg-white text-quiet hover:border-emerald-300 hover:text-forest"),children:e},e))]})]}),0===m.length?(0,a.jsxs)("p",{className:"mt-8 rounded-xl border border-dashed border-line bg-white px-4 py-8 text-center text-[12.5px] text-quiet",children:["Nothing matches “",i,"”. Try a tag like ",(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"font-mono",children:"MLOps"})," or clear the filters."]}):(0,a.jsx)("ul",{className:"mt-6 divide-y divide-line-soft border-y border-line",children:m.map(e=>{var t;return(0,a.jsx)("li",{children:(0,a.jsxs)(r(),{href:"/".concat(n,"/").concat(e.slug,"/"),className:"group grid gap-2 py-5 transition-colors hover:bg-canvas-soft/50 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] md:items-start md:gap-6 md:px-2",children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{children:[(0,a.jsxs)("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2",children:[(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"font-mono text-[9.5px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-emerald-700",children:e.category}),(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"font-mono text-[9.5px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-quiet",children:[(t=e.date,new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-GB",{day:"numeric",month:"short",year:"numeric",timeZone:"UTC"}).format(new Date(t)))," \xb7 ",e.readingMinutes," min"]})]}),(0,a.jsx)("h3",{className:"mt-1.5 text-[15.5px] font-medium leading-snug text-ink group-hover:text-forest",children:e.title}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{className:"mt-1.5 max-w-2xl text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-quiet",children:e.summary}),(0,a.jsx)("div",{className:"mt-2.5 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5",children:e.tags.map(e=>(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"rounded-full border border-line px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[9.5px] text-quiet",children:e},e))})]}),(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[11.5px] font-medium text-emerald-700 md:mt-1",children:["Read",(0,a.jsx)(l.A,{className:"h-3 w-3 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-0.5"})]})]})},e.slug)})})]})}[{slug:"zero-recall-cross-dataset",kind:"research",title:"Why the detector scores zero on a dataset it has never seen",summary:"A cross-dataset probe trained without NSL-KDD reached 0.00 recall on it. This note records what that result means, why it is published next to the 0.973 F1, and what would have to change to fix it.",category:"Detection engineering",date:"2026-01-18",readingMinutes:7,tags:["NDR","evaluation","distribution shift","security"],body:"## The result\n\nThe held-out number for the network intrusion detection model is 0.973 F1 on 44,734 flows. That number is real, and it is also the least interesting thing about the evaluation.\n\nThe interesting result is the probe: train the same pipeline without NSL-KDD, then score NSL-KDD. Recall is **0.00**. Not low. Zero. The model does not rank a single NSL-KDD attack above the threshold.\n\n## Why this is not a bug\n\nA model learns a decision boundary in the space of the features it was shown. NSL-KDD's feature semantics differ from CICIDS2017's: the same column name can carry a different scale, a different measurement window, or a different meaning entirely. When the semantics shift, the boundary lands somewhere meaningless, and a threshold tuned on the training distribution will classify everything as benign.\n\nThat is the honest description of most production drift incidents. The model is not broken. The world moved.\n\n## What the probe is actually testing\n\nThe probe answers one question: does this model transfer to a dataset it was not trained on? The answer is no, and the platform reports that answer instead of hiding it behind an aggregate.\n\nThree consequences follow:\n\n- **The model is a decision-support tool, not an unattended sensor.** That sentence belongs in the model card, not in a footnote.\n- **Monitoring has to watch inputs, not just outputs.** A drop in alert volume is the first symptom, and it looks identical to a quiet network.\n- **Retraining is a distribution question.** Adding a dataset to training helps only if the feature contract is shared, which is why the extractor lives in one module.\n\n## What I would do next\n\n1. **Align features, not columns.** Write a per-dataset adapter that maps each source onto the shared 23-feature contract, with a test per adapter.\n2. **Report per-dataset recall at a fixed false-positive budget.** A single F1 hides the shape of the failure.\n3. **Add an out-of-distribution score.** A density estimate over the feature vector would let the platform say \"this looks unlike anything I was trained on\" instead of silently predicting benign.\n\n## What this note is not\n\nThis is not a research finding about intrusion detection in general. It is a record of one measured result on one model, published so the number can be reproduced and challenged. The evaluation code and the recorded metrics are in the repository.",evidence:[{label:"NDR platform",href:c("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps")},{label:"Model card",href:"".concat(c("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps"),"/blob/main/reports/zeek_model/model_card.md")},{label:"Run the operating-point lab",href:"/labs/network-intrusion-detection/"}]},{slug:"quality-gate-rejected-retrain",kind:"research",title:"A quality gate that rejected its own retrain",summary:"The recorded medical imaging retrain reached 0.867 accuracy and 0.830 weighted F1, then was rejected because tuberculosis recall fell to 0.236. This is the arithmetic behind that decision.",category:"MLOps",date:"2026-01-09",readingMinutes:6,tags:["MLOps","model registry","evaluation","medical imaging"],body:"## The situation\n\nA retraining run finished. Accuracy was 0.867. Weighted F1 was 0.830. On a dashboard that shows two numbers, the run looks like a success.\n\nIt was not promoted.\n\n## The number that decided it\n\nThe evaluation set holds 630 images: 520 normal, 110 tuberculosis. The candidate caught 26 of the 110 positive cases. Recall on the positive class: **0.236**.\n\nThe gate's floor is 0.80. The candidate missed it by a wide margin, so the gate rejected the promotion and kept the registered baseline.\n\n## Why accuracy hid the failure\n\nWith 82.5% of the test set in the negative class, a model that predicts \"normal\" for everything scores 0.825 accuracy. The candidate's 0.867 is better than that, which is exactly the trap: aggregate accuracy is dominated by the majority class, and the minority class is the one with clinical consequences.\n\nWeighted F1 does not save you either. It weights each class by its support, so 520 correctly classified normals can outvote 84 missed cases.\n\n## The gate, in words\n\nThe gate is not clever. It checks, in order:\n\n- Is positive-class recall at or above the floor (0.80)?\n- Did accuracy drop by more than the allowed delta against the baseline?\n- Did weighted F1 regress?\n\nA candidate that fails any check is rejected with a reason attached. The reason is stored with the run so the next person does not have to guess why the registry did not move.\n\n## Why this is the interesting part of the project\n\nMost MLOps demonstrations show a pipeline promoting a better model. The promotion path is the easy half: it is plumbing. The hard half is the refusal, because a refusal requires the team to have decided in advance which metric matters and what its floor is.\n\nThat decision is the engineering. Everything else is YAML.\n\n## Limits of this result\n\n- 110 positive images is a small evaluation set; a confidence interval on 26/110 is wide.\n- The 0.80 floor is an engineering demo threshold, not a clinical standard.\n- A rejected candidate is not a failed pipeline run. It is the pipeline working.\n\nThe lab below replays the arithmetic so the numbers can be checked without reading the repository.",evidence:[{label:"Medical imaging MLOps",href:c("Medical-imaging-MLOps")},{label:"Quality gate source",href:"".concat(c("Medical-imaging-MLOps"),"/blob/main/src/automation/model_quality_gate.py")},{label:"Run the gate lab",href:"/labs/medical-imaging-mlops/"}]},{slug:"measuring-latency-in-detection",kind:"research",title:"Measuring inference latency instead of estimating it",summary:"The NDR API reports p50 2.23 ms and p95 2.92 ms over 2,000 flows. This note covers how those numbers were produced, what they exclude, and why a p95 without a percentile method is marketing.",category:"Performance",date:"2025-12-14",readingMinutes:5,tags:["latency","FastAPI","benchmarking","observability"],body:'## Why measure at all\n\n"Fast inference" is a claim. A percentile is a measurement. The difference matters because latency is the number an analyst feels when a dashboard lags behind the wire.\n\n## Method\n\nThe benchmark scores 2,000 flows through the same predictor the API uses, on the same machine, in one process. Per-flow wall time is recorded around the scoring call only. Feature extraction is measured separately because it has a different cost profile.\n\nFrom that sample:\n\n- **p50: 2.23 ms** — the typical flow\n- **p95: 2.92 ms** — the slow tail a user actually notices\n- **throughput: 432 flows/s** single-process\n\n## What these numbers exclude\n\n- **Network time.** They are server-side, before the response leaves the process.\n- **Feature extraction.** Parsing and the 23-feature transform are excluded deliberately, because mixing them into "inference" makes the number impossible to compare with anything.\n- **Concurrency.** Single-process, sequential. Under load, queueing dominates and the percentile moves.\n- **Cold start.** Model load is measured once at boot, not per request.\n\n## Why the dashboard shows latency at all\n\nThe platform stores a measured inference latency with every event. If the p95 on the dashboard drifts from the benchmark, something changed: a bigger model, a slower disk, a database lock. That is the point of publishing the baseline. It turns a vague sense that things are slower into a comparison.\n\n## The honesty rule\n\nReplay data is tagged. If the sensor is fed from a file rather than a live interface, the dashboard says so, because latency measured on replay is not latency measured on the wire.\n\nThat is the same rule as the zero-recall probe: publish the condition next to the number, or the number will be read as something it is not.',evidence:[{label:"Serving layer",href:"".concat(c("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps"),"/tree/main/src/serving")},{label:"Recorded validation",href:"".concat(c("Network-Intrusion-Detection-Advanced-MLOps"),"/blob/main/reports/serving_validation.json")}]},{slug:"what-a-statevector-simulator-hides",kind:"research",title:"What an exact statevector simulator hides",summary:"The quantum lab computes amplitudes exactly, which is both its strength and its blind spot. A note on what a noise-free simulation can and cannot tell you before you spend time on hardware.",category:"Quantum computing",date:"2025-11-22",readingMinutes:6,tags:["quantum","Qiskit","simulation","education"],body:"## The appeal of exactness\n\nA statevector simulator multiplies complex vectors by 2x2 matrices. No sampling, no noise, no queue. If the mathematics says the amplitudes are 0.7071 and 0.7071, the simulator shows exactly that, and a Bell state looks perfect.\n\nThat is useful for learning and useless for predicting hardware behaviour.\n\n## What it hides\n\n- **Gate error.** Real two-qubit gates have error rates orders of magnitude above single-qubit gates. A circuit with 20 CNOTs on today's hardware is a different circuit.\n- **Decoherence.** Amplitudes decay with time, and deep circuits take longer than coherence allows.\n- **Readout error.** Measurement itself is a noisy classical process; a measured 0 and a prepared 0 are not the same event.\n- **Connectivity.** Hardware only applies two-qubit gates between physical neighbours, so a circuit gets mapped and swapped, adding depth.\n\n## Why the lab still shows exact numbers\n\nBecause the alternative is worse. A simulated noise model that does not match the device teaches a false intuition with extra steps. The lab states plainly: this is exact linear algebra, there is no noise model, and hardware counts will differ.\n\nWhere exactness helps is checking understanding. If you build a circuit and the amplitudes disagree with your prediction, one of the two is wrong, and the simulator is not the one with the bug.\n\n## The bridge to hardware\n\nThe honest path from simulation to hardware is:\n\n1. Predict the outcome analytically.\n2. Confirm it on a simulator.\n3. Run it on hardware with a transpiled circuit and read the counts.\n4. Compare the counts to the prediction and attribute the difference to error, not to mystery.\n\nStep 4 is where most tutorials stop, which is why most tutorials leave people confused about why their Bell state comes back 0.85/0.15 instead of 0.5/0.5.\n\n## Related work\n\nThe Qiskit repositories linked below implement the same circuits for real: Shor, Grover, phase estimation, teleportation, superdense coding and quantum kernels. They are notebooks, not claims. Open one and run it.",evidence:[{label:"Quantum lab",href:"/quantum-lab/"},{label:"Shor's algorithm",href:c("Shor-s-Algorithm")},{label:"Grover's search",href:c("Grover_Algorithm")}]},...d].sort((e,t)=>Date.parse(t.date)-Date.parse(e.date))}}]);